# Explicit `any` type annotation — the TypeScript escape hatch that opts a
# value out of type checking entirely.
#
# From work-epub/backend/typescript-training/typescript-training.md (the team's
# TS training material): `any` = "Opt-out of type checking (disables safety)",
# recommended only "when migrating code ... (avoid if possible)". Its safe twin
# `unknown` "forces type narrowing before use". An `any` param or return value
# silently disables inference for every caller downstream, so a single `any`
# erodes safety far beyond the annotated symbol. typescript-eslint's
# `no-explicit-any` targets exactly this.
#
# Why a NEW rule (verified false-negative on mestjs slop.service.ts,
# 2026-09-26): `coerce(input: any): any` was flagged by no existing rule; no
# KEUR-TS-* rule existed.
#
# HOW: match a type-annotation position `: any` — a colon, optional
# whitespace, the word `any`, then a boundary (`)`, `,`, `;`, `=`, `{`,
# end-of-line, or `[]` for `any[]`). Anchoring on `:` plus a trailing boundary
# avoids matching the word "any" in prose, identifiers like `many`/`anyone`
# (word-boundary), or `Company:` style keys (requires the value to BE `any`).
#
# RE2-safe: a single bounded `\s*` and a fixed alternation for the trailing
# boundary; no backreferences or nested unbounded quantifiers. WARN, not block:
# `any` is sometimes a deliberate, reviewed migration choice.
#
# @see work-epub typescript-training.md (type table: any = disables safety);
#      typescript-eslint no-explicit-any.
id: KEUR-TS-001
maturity: 1
title: Explicit `any` type annotation — opts out of type checking
category: quality
severity: warning
engine: pattern
action: warn
skip_comments: true
target:
  extensions: .ts .tsx
  exclude_paths: node_modules/ dist/ build/ vendor/
patterns:
  # `: any` in annotation position, followed by a type-boundary token.
  - regex: :\s*any\b(?:\[\])?\s*(?:[),;={]|$)
    message: "Explicit `any` disables type checking for this value and every downstream caller (ts-training: 'opt-out of type checking, disables safety'). Prefer `unknown` (forces narrowing) or a concrete type. @see KEUR-TS-001"
confidence: 0.75
fix: "Replace `any` with a concrete type, or `unknown` when the shape is genuinely dynamic (then narrow with a type guard before use). Reserve `any` for reviewed, temporary migration code and annotate why."
